\title[First-Order Linear ODEs with EML Coefficients]{First-Order Linear ODEs with Exponential--Logarithmic Coefficients: A Constructive Solution Calculus and Its Infinitesimal Uniqueness Law}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the positive, constructive theory of first-order linear ordinary
differential equations $y' = c(x)\,y$ whose coefficient $c$ is an
\emph{exponential--logarithmic} (EML) function---one built from the transcendental
operations $\exp$ and $\log$. The cornerstone is a single master construction: if
$F$ is an antiderivative of $c$ (that is, $F' = c$), then $y = \exp \circ F$ solves
the equation, with derivative computed exactly by the chain rule. We specialize
this to the three archetypal EML coefficient classes---the logarithmic coefficient
$c(x) = \log x$, solved by the continuous Stirling exponent
$y(x) = \exp(x\log x - x)$; the exponential coefficient $c(x) = \exp x$, solved by
the double exponential $y(x) = \exp(\exp x)$; and the power / inverse-linear
coefficient $c(x) = a/x$, solved by $y(x) = \exp(a\log x) = x^a$. We then prove an
infinitesimal uniqueness-up-to-a-constant law: any solution divided by the
canonical solution $\exp \circ F$ has vanishing derivative, so the solution space
is one-dimensional over the constants. The algebraic engine underlying every result
is the logarithmic derivative $L(y) = y'/y$, a homomorphism from the multiplicative
group of a differential field to its additive group; solving $y' = c\,y$ is exactly
solving $L(y) = c$. Every statement is backed by a machine-checked formal proof in
the Lean/Mathlib calculus library. These positive results complement an existing
negative (obstruction) theory for second-order EML equations, of which Airy's
equation $y'' = x\,y$ is the prototype.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The equation $y' = c\,y$ is the universal model of proportional change. When $c$ is
a scalar, the solution $y = K e^{cx}$ is elementary. When $c$ is a function of $x$,
the solution is still elementary in principle---$y = K \exp(\int c)$---but its
character depends sharply on the analytic nature of $c$. We isolate the case where
$c$ is \emph{exponential--logarithmic}: assembled from $\exp$, $\log$, the field
operations, and constants. This class is large enough to contain genuinely
transcendental, non-Liouvillian growth, yet structured enough to admit a complete
first-order solution calculus.

Our contribution is fourfold:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a master construction (Theorem~\ref{thm:master}) reducing the solution of
  any first-order linear equation to antidifferentiation of its coefficient, via
  $y = \exp \circ F$;
  \item closed-form solutions for the three archetypal EML coefficient classes
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:log}--\ref{thm:power}), each obtained from one antiderivative;
  \item an infinitesimal uniqueness law (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq}) showing the
  solution space is one-dimensional over constants;
  \item identification of the logarithmic derivative homomorphism $L(y) = y'/y$
  (Section~\ref{sec:engine}) as the algebraic mechanism unifying the above.
\end{enumerate}

All results are formalized; the formal statements use Mathlib's \texttt{HasDerivAt}
predicate, which asserts pointwise differentiability with a specified derivative
value, so each theorem is a verified analytic identity.

\subsection{Setting and notation}
Throughout, $\exp$ and $\log$ are the real exponential and natural logarithm. For
$y : \R \to \R$ we write $y'(x)$ for the derivative; formally ``$y$ has derivative
$v$ at $x$'' is \texttt{HasDerivAt y v x}. The logarithm is differentiable exactly
on $\R\setminus\{0\}$ with $(\log)'(x) = 1/x$, the source of the $x>0$ hypotheses
below (which are load-bearing). For Section~\ref{sec:engine} we work in a
differential field $K$: a field with a derivation $\cdot'$ satisfying additivity and
the Leibniz rule $(yz)' = y'z + yz'$. The constants $\{x \in K : x' = 0\}$ form a
subfield.

\section{The algebraic engine: the logarithmic derivative homomorphism}
\label{sec:engine}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic derivative]
For a nonzero element $y$ of a differential field $K$, the \emph{logarithmic
derivative} is $L(y) = y'/y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Homomorphism law]
\label{prop:hom}
$L$ is a group homomorphism from the multiplicative group $K^\times$ to the additive
group $(K,+)$:
\[
  L(yz) = L(y) + L(z), \qquad L(y^{-1}) = -L(y), \qquad L(y/z) = L(y) - L(z),
\]
and $L(y^n) = n\,L(y)$ for $n \in \Z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the Leibniz rule, $(yz)' = y'z + yz'$, hence
$L(yz) = (y'z + yz')/(yz) = y'/y + z'/z = L(y) + L(z)$. The remaining identities
follow from the homomorphism property.
\end{proof}

This is the conceptual core. Solving $y' = c\,y$ is literally solving $L(y) = c$.
Proposition~\ref{prop:hom} explains the closure of the EML solution class: products,
quotients, inverses, and integer powers of solutions correspond under $L$ to sums,
differences, negatives, and multiples of coefficients. The kernel of $L$ is the
units of the constants subfield, which is why solutions are unique only up to a
constant (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq}).

\section{The master construction}

\begin{theorem}[Master construction; \texttt{hasDerivAt\_exp\_comp\_solves}]
\label{thm:master}
Let $F : \R \to \R$ and $c, x \in \R$. If $F$ has derivative $c$ at $x$, then
$t \mapsto \exp(F(t))$ has derivative $c \cdot \exp(F(x))$ at $x$. Equivalently, if
$F' = c$ then $\exp \circ F$ solves $y' = c\,y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The chain rule for the exponential (\texttt{HasDerivAt.exp}) gives that
$\exp \circ F$ has derivative $\exp(F(x)) \cdot c$ at $x$. Commuting the product
yields $c \cdot \exp(F(x)) = c \cdot y(x)$ for $y = \exp \circ F$.
\end{proof}

The lemma converts the analytic problem (solve an ODE) into the symbolic problem
(antidifferentiate the coefficient). For EML coefficients the antiderivative is
again EML, and Theorems~\ref{thm:log}--\ref{thm:power} are each one antiderivative
computation.

\section{Closed-form solutions for the three EML coefficient classes}

\subsection{Logarithmic coefficient}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic-coefficient ODE; \texttt{solves\_log\_coeff}]
\label{thm:log}
For every $x > 0$, $y(x) = \exp(x\log x - x)$ has derivative
$(\log x)\,\exp(x\log x - x)$; that is, it solves $y' = (\log x)\,y$ on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $F(t) = t\log t - t$. By the product rule, $t \mapsto t\log t$ has derivative
$1\cdot\log x + x\cdot x^{-1}$ at $x$ (using $(\log)'(x) = x^{-1}$). Subtracting the
identity (derivative $1$) gives $F'(x) = \log x + x\cdot x^{-1} - 1$. Since
$x \ne 0$, $x\cdot x^{-1} = 1$, so $F'(x) = \log x$. Apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:master}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Stirling exponent]
The exponent $x\log x - x$ is the continuous Stirling exponent: $n! \sim
\sqrt{2\pi n}\,\exp(n\log n - n)$. Thus $y' = (\log x)\,y$ is the leading-order law
of factorial / Gamma growth, with a genuinely transcendental solution (not algebraic
over $\R(x)$).
\end{remark}

\subsection{Exponential coefficient}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential-coefficient ODE; \texttt{solves\_exp\_coeff}]
\label{thm:exp}
For every $x \in \R$, $y(x) = \exp(\exp x)$ has derivative
$(\exp x)\,\exp(\exp x)$; that is, it solves $y' = (\exp x)\,y$ everywhere.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $F(t) = \exp t$, with $F'(x) = \exp x$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:master}. No
domain restriction is needed since $\exp$ is everywhere differentiable.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The double exponential is the Gompertz growth function, central to actuarial
mortality and tumor-growth models.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Power / inverse-linear coefficient}

\begin{theorem}[Power-coefficient ODE; \texttt{solves\_power\_coeff}]
\label{thm:power}
For every $x > 0$ and every $a \in \R$, $y(x) = \exp(a\log x) = x^a$ has derivative
$(a/x)\,\exp(a\log x)$; that is, it solves $y' = (a/x)\,y$ on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $F(t) = a\log t$, with $F'(x) = a\cdot x^{-1} = a/x$. Apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:master}. Since $\exp(a\log x) = x^a$ for $x>0$, the solution is the
power function.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This recovers the scaling laws $x^a$ as EML solutions of the rational coefficient
$a/x = a\,(\log x)'$. For rational $a$ the solution is algebraic over $\R(x)$; for
irrational $a$ it is transcendental.
\end{remark}

\section{Infinitesimal uniqueness up to a constant}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness up to a constant; \texttt{solution\_ratio\_hasDerivAt\_zero}]
\label{thm:uniq}
Let $y, F : \R \to \R$ and $c, x \in \R$. If $y$ has derivative $c\cdot y(x)$ at $x$
(i.e.\ $y$ solves $y' = c\,y$ at $x$) and $F$ has derivative $c$ at $x$, then
$t \mapsto y(t)/\exp(F(t))$ has derivative $0$ at $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the chain rule, $z(t) = \exp(F(t))$ has derivative $\exp(F(x))\cdot c$ at $x$,
and $z(x) \ne 0$. The quotient rule gives the derivative of $y/z$ as
\[
  \frac{c\,y(x)\,\exp(F(x)) - y(x)\,(\exp(F(x))\,c)}{\exp(F(x))^2} = 0,
\]
since the numerator cancels.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A function with everywhere-zero derivative on a connected domain is constant; hence
every solution of $y' = c\,y$ equals $K\cdot\exp(F)$ for a constant $K$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq} is the infinitesimal core of this and the analytic
counterpart of the algebraic result \texttt{firstOrder\_ratio\_isConstant}: if
$y_1' = a\,y_1$, $y_2' = a\,y_2$ and $y_2 \ne 0$, then $(y_1/y_2)' = 0$. The
solution line of a first-order EML equation is one-dimensional over the constants.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{EML coefficient solver via antiderivative exponentiation}
Given an EML coefficient $c$ and an antiderivative $F$ with $F' = c$, return
$\exp \circ F$ with verified derivative $c\cdot\exp(F)$. For the three archetypes
$F$ is selected from the table $\log \mapsto x\log x - x$, $\exp \mapsto \exp$,
$a/x \mapsto a\log x$, realizing Theorem~\ref{thm:master}.

\subsection{Numerical solution verifier}
Verify $y' = c\,y$ via the central difference $y'(x) \approx (y(x+h)-y(x-h))/(2h)$
and the residual $|y'(x) - c(x)y(x)|$, confirming
Theorems~\ref{thm:log}--\ref{thm:power}.

\subsection{Uniqueness-ratio constancy check}
Form the ratio of two numerical solutions of the same equation and verify it is
constant across the domain, confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq}.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Factorial asymptotics.} Theorem~\ref{thm:log} ties $y'=(\log x)y$
  to the Stirling exponent $x\log x - x$.
  \item \textbf{Growth/decay models.} Theorem~\ref{thm:exp}'s double exponential is
  the Gompertz law; Theorem~\ref{thm:power}'s powers are scaling laws.
  \item \textbf{Symbolic ODE solving.} Theorem~\ref{thm:master} is a verified
  decision procedure for coefficients with a named antiderivative, and
  Proposition~\ref{prop:hom} delineates which coefficients keep solutions EML.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}
These results are the positive half of an EML differential-equations program. The
complementary negative half studies when EML solutions fail to exist: the canonical
example is Airy's equation $y'' = x\,y$, which has no EML-elementary solution, by a
degree/valuation obstruction in $\R[x,\log x]$ via the Riccati transform
$v = y'/y \Rightarrow v' + v^2 = x$. The logarithmic derivative is the hinge of both
halves.

\section{Future directions}
\textbf{C1.} Upgrade Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq} to global uniqueness: on a connected
domain every $C^1$ solution equals $K\cdot\exp(F)$ for constant $K$.\\
\textbf{C2.} Second-order dichotomy: conjecture $y'' = (\log x)y$ has no
$\exp(F)$ solution with $F$ polynomial in $x,\log x$, via $v' + v^2 = \log x$.\\
\textbf{C3.} Conjecture $\Gamma(x+1)$ and $\exp(x\log x - x)$ share the same
logarithmic derivative asymptotically: $\logDeriv\Gamma(x+1) - \log x \to 0$.\\
\textbf{C4.} Conjecture $\ker L$ equals the units of the constants subfield, so $L$
descends to an injective $K^\times/\text{constants}^\times \hookrightarrow (K,+)$.

\section{Conclusion}
Reducing first-order linear EML differential equations to antidifferentiation, we
obtained verified closed-form solutions for the logarithmic, exponential, and power
coefficient classes, and an infinitesimal uniqueness law placing the solution space
on a single line over the constants. The unifying mechanism is the logarithmic
derivative homomorphism, the differential incarnation of the duality between
multiplication and addition that binds the exponential and the logarithm.

\end{document}

